.\" Man page for sshcheck
.\" Contact: rom@romab.com
.TH SSHCHECK 1 "February 2026" "sshcheck 5.0.0" "Security Audit Tools"
.SH NAME
sshcheck \- SSH Security Audit Client for testing login capabilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sshcheck
[\fB\-t\fR \fIHOST\fR | \fB\-T\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR \fIUSER\fR | \fB\-U\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIPASS\fR | \fB\-P\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-k\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-K\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-C\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-try\-empty\fR]
[\fB\-\-user\-as\-pass\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR | \fB\-\-port-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-proxy\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-color\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR \fICMD\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-spray\fR]
[\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fIHOST\fR | \fB\-\-exclude-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-detect\-honeypot\fR]
[\fB\-\-known\-hosts\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-import\-nmap\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-source\-ip\fR \fIIP\fR]
[\fB\-\-jitter\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-scan\-ports\fR]
[\fB\-\-score\-passwords\fR]
[\fB\-\-diff\fR]
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshcheck
is a security audit tool designed for testing SSH login capabilities across multiple hosts.
It attempts to authenticate to target SSH servers using provided credentials and captures
initial server output including welcome messages, banners, and shell prompts.
.PP
The tool provides host key fingerprinting, SSH algorithm enumeration with weak algorithm
detection, OS/version fingerprinting from SSH banners, automatic severity scoring of
findings, post-authentication command execution, and multiple output formats including
styled HTML reports.
.PP
This tool is intended for authorized penetration testing, security assessments, and
network audits. Always ensure you have proper authorization before scanning any systems.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Configuration"
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Load configuration from YAML or JSON file. All scan parameters can be specified
in the config file. CLI arguments override config file values.
.SS "Target Specification"
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target " " \fIHOST\fR
Target host(s) to scan. Can be specified multiple times. Accepts:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Single IP address (e.g., 192.168.1.1)
.IP \(bu 2
CIDR range (e.g., 192.168.1.0/24)
.IP \(bu 2
IP range (e.g., 192.168.1.1-254)
.IP \(bu 2
Hostname (e.g., server.example.com)
.RE
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-target-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing target hosts, one per line. Supports the same formats as \fB-t\fR.
Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Username Specification"
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fR
Username(s) to try. Can be specified multiple times for multiple usernames.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-user-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing usernames, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Password Specification"
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-password " " \fIPASS\fR
Password(s) to try. Can be specified multiple times for multiple passwords.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-password-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing passwords, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.TP
.BR \-\-try\-empty
Also try an empty/null password for each username. Useful for detecting accounts
that allow passwordless login.
.TP
.BR \-\-user\-as\-pass
Also try the username as the password for each user. Tests for the common
misconfiguration where the password equals the username.
.SS "Key Authentication"
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIFILE\fR
SSH private key file(s) for key-based authentication. Supports RSA, Ed25519,
ECDSA, and DSA keys. Can be specified multiple times. Use \fB-p\fR to provide
a passphrase for encrypted keys.
.TP
.BR \-K ", " \-\-key-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing paths to SSH private key files, one per line. Lines starting
with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Combo File"
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-combo-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing username:password combinations, one per line. Format:
username:password. Colons in passwords are supported (splits on first colon).
Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Port Specification"
.TP
.BR \-\-port " " \fIPORT\fR
SSH port(s) to try. Default is 22. Can be specified multiple times.
.TP
.BR \-\-port-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing ports, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Output Options"
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Save results to the specified file. The output format is determined by the \fB-f\fR option.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format for the results file. Valid options:
.RS
.IP \fBtext\fR
Human-readable text format (default)
.IP \fBjson\fR
JSON format with full scan details, host key info, algorithms, and severity
.IP \fBcsv\fR
Comma-separated values for spreadsheet import
.IP \fBxml\fR
Structured XML format for tool integration
.IP \fBhtml\fR
Styled HTML report with summary cards, severity badges, and color coding
.IP \fBpdf\fR
PDF report with scan statistics, successful logins, and all results (no external dependencies)
.RE
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output with detailed error messages and debug information.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output, only show final summary.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-color
Disable colored terminal output. Useful for piping output to files or
when using terminals without color support.
.SS "Scan Control"
.TP
.BR \-\-stop\-on\-success
Stop testing a host:port combination after the first successful login is found.
Saves time on large scans where only one valid credential per host is needed.
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-attempts\-per\-user " " \fINUM\fR
Maximum number of failed password attempts per username per host before skipping
further attempts for that user. Protects against account lockout on systems with
lockout policies. Set to 0 for unlimited (default).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Execute the specified command on each successful login and capture the output.
The command is run via exec_command. When this option is used, the initial shell
output capture is skipped in favor of command output.
.TP
.BR \-\-checkpoint " " \fIFILE\fR
Save scan progress to a JSON checkpoint file. Progress is saved periodically
during scanning and when the scan is interrupted with Ctrl+C.
.TP
.BR \-\-resume " " \fIFILE\fR
Resume a previously interrupted scan from a checkpoint file. Already completed
attempts are skipped and previous results are restored.
.TP
.BR \-\-spray
Enable credential spray mode. Tries one password across all users and hosts
before moving to the next password. This helps avoid account lockouts in
enterprise environments with lockout policies.
.SS "Target Exclusion"
.TP
.BR \-\-exclude " " \fIHOST\fR
Host(s) to exclude from scanning. Accepts the same formats as \fB-t\fR:
single IP, CIDR range, IP range, or hostname. Can be specified multiple times.
.TP
.BR \-\-exclude\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing hosts to exclude, one per line. Same format as target files.
.SS "Security Features"
.TP
.BR \-\-detect\-honeypot
Enable honeypot detection. Analyzes SSH banners, response patterns, timing,
and behavior for signs of SSH honeypots such as Cowrie and Kippo. Results
include a honeypot score (0.0-1.0) and specific indicator reasons.
.TP
.BR \-\-known\-hosts " " \fIFILE\fR
Check host keys against a known hosts file for MITM (Man-in-the-Middle)
detection. Supports OpenSSH known_hosts format and sshcheck's own JSON
format. Discovered host keys are saved back to this file after scanning
for future comparison.
.TP
.BR \-\-baseline " " \fIFILE\fR
Compare scan results against a previous scan baseline in JSON format.
Reports new/removed hosts, changed credentials, host key changes, and
SSH version changes. A diff file (.diff.json) is saved alongside the
output file.
.SS "Nmap Integration"
.TP
.BR \-\-import\-nmap " " \fIFILE\fR
Import targets from an Nmap XML output file (\fB-oX\fR). Extracts hosts
with open SSH ports (port 22, 2222, or any port with service name 'ssh').
Can be combined with \fB-t\fR for additional targets.
.SS "Network Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-source\-ip " " \fIIP\fR
Bind to a specific source IP address for outgoing connections.
Useful for testing from different network interfaces or VLANs.
.SS "Service Discovery"
.TP
.BR \-\-scan\-ports
Discover SSH services before scanning. Performs a TCP connect scan
on common SSH ports (22, 2222, 2200, etc.) to find SSH services.
Discovered ports are added to the scan target list.
.TP
.BR \-\-discovery\-ports " " \fIPORTS\fR
Comma-separated list of ports to check during service discovery.
Default: 22,2222,2200,22222,8022,830,222,2022,2220,10022
.SS "Differential Output"
.TP
.BR \-\-diff
Enable differential/delta output mode. When used with \fB\-\-baseline\fR,
the output file contains only changes between scans instead of the full report.
Supports JSON, CSV, and text output formats.
.SS "Password Analysis"
.TP
.BR \-\-score\-passwords
Score password strength for successful logins. Evaluates length, character
diversity, common patterns, and entropy. Reports a 0-100 score with labels
(very_weak, weak, moderate, strong, very_strong).
.SS "Performance Options"
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-threads " " \fINUM\fR
Number of concurrent threads. Default is 1 (sequential scanning).
Higher values increase scan speed but may trigger rate limiting.
Recommended range: 1-50.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
Connection timeout in seconds. Default is 10 seconds.
Increase for slow networks or decrease for faster scanning.
.TP
.BR \-\-jitter " " \fISECONDS\fR
Add random delay (0 to SECONDS) between connection attempts.
Helps avoid rate limiting and IDS detection. Default: 0 (no jitter).
.SS "Proxy Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-proxy " " \fIURL\fR
Route all connections through a proxy. Supported formats:
.RS
.IP \fBsocks5://host:port\fR
SOCKS5 proxy (requires PySocks library)
.IP \fBsocks4://host:port\fR
SOCKS4 proxy (requires PySocks library)
.IP \fBhttp://host:port\fR
HTTP CONNECT proxy
.RE
.SS "Other Options"
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.SH SECURITY FEATURES
.SS "Host Key Fingerprinting"
For each successful connection, sshcheck collects the server's host key type
(e.g., ssh-ed25519, ssh-rsa), SHA-256 fingerprint, and key size in bits.
Weak host keys such as DSA and short RSA keys are flagged in the severity assessment.
.SS "Algorithm Enumeration"
The tool reports available key exchange, cipher, MAC, and host key algorithms.
Weak algorithms are identified, including: RC4/arcfour ciphers, CBC mode ciphers,
MD5 and SHA-1 based MACs, DSA host keys, and weak Diffie-Hellman groups.
.SS "OS/Version Fingerprinting"
SSH banners are parsed to identify the operating system (Ubuntu, Debian, FreeBSD,
CentOS, Windows, etc.), OS family, and SSH software version. Known vulnerable
OpenSSH versions are flagged with appropriate severity levels.
.SS "Honeypot Detection"
When enabled with \fB\-\-detect\-honeypot\fR, sshcheck analyzes connections for
honeypot indicators: known Cowrie/Kippo banners, suspicious response patterns,
default honeypot hostnames, unusually fast connections, and trivial credential
acceptance. Each result receives a score from 0.0 to 1.0.
.SS "Host Key Continuity"
The \fB\-\-known\-hosts\fR option monitors host keys across scans to detect
potential MITM attacks. Host key changes are flagged with CRITICAL severity.
Supports both OpenSSH known_hosts format and sshcheck's JSON format.
.SS "Baseline Comparison"
The \fB\-\-baseline\fR option compares current results against a previous scan
to identify: new/removed hosts, new/lost credentials, host key changes, and
SSH version changes. This enables continuous security monitoring.
.SS "Credential Spraying"
The \fB\-\-spray\fR option changes the credential testing order to try one
password across all users and hosts before moving to the next password. This
avoids triggering account lockout policies in enterprise environments.
.SS "Nmap Integration"
The \fB\-\-import\-nmap\fR option reads Nmap XML output to automatically
target only hosts with confirmed open SSH ports, integrating into existing
network discovery workflows.
.SS "Source IP Binding"
The \fB\-\-source\-ip\fR option binds all outgoing connections to a specific
local IP address, useful for multi-homed machines or testing from specific VLANs.
.SS "Jitter/Randomization"
The \fB\-\-jitter\fR option adds random delay between attempts to avoid
IDS detection and rate limiting. The delay is uniformly distributed between
0 and the specified number of seconds.
.SS "Password Strength Scoring"
When \fB\-\-score\-passwords\fR is enabled, each successful login receives
a password strength score (0-100) based on length, character diversity,
entropy, common password lists, and pattern analysis.
.SS "Service Discovery"
The \fB\-\-scan\-ports\fR option performs TCP connect scanning on common SSH
ports before the main credential scan, automatically adding discovered SSH
services to the target list.
.SS "Differential Output"
The \fB\-\-diff\fR option (used with \fB\-\-baseline\fR) outputs only changes
between scans instead of full reports, ideal for monitoring pipelines.
.SS "SSH Key Authentication"
The \fB\-k\fR option supports testing with SSH private keys. Key types are
auto-detected (RSA, Ed25519, ECDSA, DSA). Encrypted keys require a
passphrase via \fB\-p\fR. Multiple keys can be tested per host.
.SS "Combo File Support"
The \fB\-C\fR option loads pre-matched username:password pairs from a file,
useful for testing leaked credentials or database dumps.
.SS "Proxy Support"
The \fB\-\-proxy\fR option tunnels all SSH connections through SOCKS4,
SOCKS5, or HTTP CONNECT proxies. Useful for scanning through Tor,
corporate proxies, or bastion hosts.
.SS "CVE Vulnerability Database"
A built-in database checks SSH server versions against known CVEs including
CVE-2024-6387 (regreSSHion), CVE-2023-48795 (Terrapin Attack),
CVE-2023-38408 (ssh-agent RCE), and others for OpenSSH, Dropbear, and libssh.
.SS "Color Output"
Terminal output uses colored text for readability. Green for success, red for
failures and critical severity, yellow for warnings, cyan for progress.
Disable with \fB\-\-no\-color\fR for piped output.
.SS "Progress Bar"
A visual progress bar with ETA is displayed during scanning, showing
completion percentage, elapsed time, and estimated remaining time.
.SS "PDF Reports"
The \fB\-f pdf\fR option generates self-contained PDF security audit reports
with no external Python dependencies.
.SS "Severity Scoring"
Each finding is automatically assessed for severity:
.TP
\fBCRITICAL\fR \- Root/admin login with default, empty, or username-as-password credentials.
.TP
\fBHIGH\fR \- Successful login for non-privileged users.
.TP
\fBMEDIUM\fR \- Weak host keys (DSA, short RSA), vulnerable SSH versions.
.TP
\fBLOW\fR \- Weak algorithms supported (RC4, 3DES, MD5-based MACs).
.TP
\fBINFO\fR \- No significant security findings.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Configuration files support all scan parameters. CLI arguments override config values.
.PP
JSON example:
.PP
.nf
{
  "targets": ["192.168.1.0/24"],
  "users": ["root", "admin"],
  "passwords": ["password", "changeme"],
  "threads": 10,
  "try_empty": true,
  "user_as_pass": true,
  "stop_on_success": true,
  "max_attempts_per_user": 3,
  "command": "id; uname -a",
  "format": "html",
  "output": "report.html"
}
.fi
.PP
YAML configuration files require the \fBpyyaml\fR package.
.SH INPUT FILE FORMATS
All input files (targets, usernames, passwords, ports) support:
.IP \(bu 2
One entry per line
.IP \(bu 2
Lines starting with # are treated as comments
.IP \(bu 2
Empty lines are ignored
.IP \(bu 2
Leading and trailing whitespace is stripped
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one successful login was found.
.TP
.B 1
No successful logins found or error occurred.
.TP
.B 130
Scan was interrupted by user (Ctrl+C). Checkpoint is saved if configured.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Scan single host with single credentials:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u admin -p password123
.TP
Test empty passwords and username-as-password:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 --try-empty --user-as-pass -u root -u admin
.TP
Execute command on success with HTML report:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass -c "id; uname -a" -f html -o report.html
.TP
Scan subnet with lockout protection:
.B sshcheck -t 10.0.0.0/24 -U users.txt -P passwords.txt -n 10 --max-attempts-per-user 3
.TP
Scan with checkpoint and resume:
.B sshcheck -T targets.txt -U users.txt -P passwords.txt --checkpoint scan.json
.br
.B sshcheck -T targets.txt -U users.txt -P passwords.txt --resume scan.json
.TP
Use configuration file:
.B sshcheck --config scan_profile.yaml
.TP
Credential spray mode:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -U users.txt -P passwords.txt --spray -n 5
.TP
Scan with host exclusions:
.B sshcheck -t 10.0.0.0/24 --exclude 10.0.0.1 --exclude 10.0.0.254 -u root -p pass
.TP
Import targets from Nmap and scan:
.B sshcheck --import-nmap scan.xml -U users.txt -P passwords.txt
.TP
Honeypot detection:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass --detect-honeypot
.TP
MITM detection with known hosts:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -u root -p pass --known-hosts hosts.json
.TP
Baseline comparison:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -u root -p pass --baseline prev_scan.json -o current.json -f json
.TP
Scan with source IP binding:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -u root -p pass --source-ip 10.0.0.5
.TP
Jitter between attempts:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -U users.txt -P passwords.txt --jitter 2.0
.TP
Password strength scoring with PDF report:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -U users.txt -P passwords.txt --score-passwords -f pdf -o report.pdf
.TP
SSH port discovery before scanning:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 --scan-ports -U users.txt -P passwords.txt
.TP
Differential output (changes only):
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -u root -p pass --baseline prev.json --diff -o changes.json -f json
.TP
SSH key authentication:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u admin -k ~/.ssh/id_rsa
.TP
Multiple key files:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u admin -k ~/.ssh/id_rsa -k ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
.TP
Combo file scanning:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -C combos.txt -o results.json -f json
.TP
Scan through SOCKS5 proxy (Tor):
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass --proxy socks5://127.0.0.1:9050
.TP
Scan through HTTP proxy:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass --proxy http://proxy.example.com:8080
.TP
Disable color output:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -u root -p pass --no-color
.SH ERROR MESSAGES
.TP
.B "Authentication failed"
The username/password combination was rejected by the server.
.TP
.B "Connection timed out"
The host did not respond within the timeout period.
.TP
.B "Connection refused"
SSH service may not be running on the specified port.
.TP
.B "No route to host"
Network path to the target is unavailable.
.TP
.B "Network is unreachable"
Cannot reach the target network.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I examples/targets.txt
Example target hosts file
.TP
.I examples/users.txt
Example usernames file
.TP
.I examples/passwords.txt
Example passwords file
.TP
.I examples/scan_profile.json
Example JSON configuration file
.TP
.I examples/combos.txt
Example combo file (username:password pairs)
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.PP
This tool is designed for authorized security testing only.
Unauthorized use against systems you do not own or have explicit
permission to test may violate computer crime laws.
.PP
Passwords provided on the command line may be visible in process
listings. Use password files (\fB-P\fR) for sensitive credentials.
.PP
Checkpoint files contain scan results including passwords. Store
them securely and delete after use.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Python 3.6 or later
.IP \(bu 2
paramiko >= 2.7.0 (pip install paramiko)
.IP \(bu 2
pyyaml >= 5.0.0 (optional, for YAML config files)
.IP \(bu 2
pysocks >= 1.7.0 (optional, for SOCKS proxy support)
